00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.757
So why should you care
about deep learning?

00:03:08.757 --> 00:03:11.340
A quick primer, there's a lot
of sessions around deep learning

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.510
and TensorFlow here.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.051
We're going to go through some
of the suggested sessions that

00:03:15.051 --> 00:03:17.010
are still available
later today and tomorrow.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.690
But deep learning
is another machine

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.900
learning technique alongside
regression, classification,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.100
and clustering.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.900
The real nuance
behind this is that we

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.150
have a lot of
hidden layers within

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.130
artificial neural
networks that allow you

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.390
to model a lot more complexity.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:34.320
Why is this fundamentally
different from traditional ML

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.310
techniques?

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.860
The more data you give
it, the better it gets.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.500
Other techniques tend
to have a plateau.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.850
But with deep learning,
the more data you collect,

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:44.580
the more examples you
feed it, we really

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.490
are seeing breakthroughs
in accuracy.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.810
And really two applications
you want to think about.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:53.520
Number one is new domains
for traditional R usage,

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.190
breaking into what we
call perception services--

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.380
vision, natural
language, speech,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.830
when we talk about the
ability to diagnose diseases

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.800
within medical imagery, when we
talk about identifying product

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.320
quality defects on
manufacturing lines,

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.610
being able to classify
product reviews automatically.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.280
There's a whole new
use cases for that.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.339
But second, let's not forget
about our traditional structure

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:18.089
use cases.

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:19.620
I really believe
deep learning has

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.070
a place for specific niches
around sequential data

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.560
to actually drive more
value and squeeze out

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.150
the actual accuracy.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.600
And then also for very heavy
feature engineering, also known

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.190
as veritable enrichment,
deep learning

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.830
can really help to
speed up that as well.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:40.410
Now, if you're convinced about
deep learning, why TensorFlow?

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.150
Just a couple of
quick bullets on it.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.660
It is a numerical computation
library, allows you to run

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.610
operations in parallel.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.010
This really allows you to
distribute large machine

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.380
learning training
jobs across machines.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.780
As R users, were
usually restrained

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.370
to the RAM of a machine.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.170
With TensorFlow's
framework, we really

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.960
are able to now leverage big
data in the machine learning

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.260
space.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.310
And then finally, TensorFlow
is a growing community

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.740
and an ecosystem where
we're open sourcing

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.900
not just algorithms,
but actual reference

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.240
architectures that you can
start using immediately

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.900
without architecting these
neural networks from scratch.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.830
And then the third and final
piece is why Google Cloud?

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.590
We want you to focus
on your R code.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:25.530
We don't want you to worry about
spinning up infrastructure.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.720
We don't want you to
maintain these clusters.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.850
We really want you
to focus on the code,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.430
deploy it into managed
services, whether that

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.870
means you're trying to
store millions of images,

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.990
audio files, machine
logs, whether you're

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.210
trying to query
petabytes of data,

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.700
we really want you to use
Google Cloud Storage or Google

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.954
BigQuery that you've heard
about in other sessions as well.

00:05:44.954 --> 00:05:46.620
And then the last
piece, which is really

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.110
what I'm really close to is
really speeding up the time

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.910
to operationalize models.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.690
Through traditional data
science workflow with R

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.960
really treats R as a data
science experimentation place,

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.720
and then you need to really work
with IT to productionalize it.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.219
We'll go through
some examples where

00:06:02.219 --> 00:06:05.400
we're able to deploy those
models directly for consumers

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.790
and developers to consume
your models in API form.

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.530
And finally I think the most--

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.180
really the most
important piece is

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.970
we're excited to bring the R
community to the deep learning

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:16.889
world.

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:18.430
Here's a great quote
