00:11:39.492 --> 00:11:41.450
Everything you can do in
Python with TensorFlow

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:43.400
you can do in R, 100% coverage.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:47.300
But TensorFlow itself is
quite a low-level programming

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:48.170
environment.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.900
You basically have to write
some mathematical equations

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:51.960
to make use of that.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.860
So I see some people
nodding their heads.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:59.000
The much more sensible thing
to do as a practitioner

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.650
is to use Keras, which
is a higher-level wrapper

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.020
library around TensorFlow.

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:09.590
Now, the Keras package also
available on cron, again,

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:14.860
is 100% coverage of the
Keras library in Python.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:17.330
So again, everything you
can do in Keras on Python

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:21.110
you can do in R. And that's
the one I would recommend you

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:25.490
use in most of your day-to-day
data science exploratory work.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:33.680
TF Estimators is a package that
is much more targeted at a use

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:35.780
case where you have
large amounts of data,

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:38.390
you have simple models,
and you want to take that

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:39.764
into production very quickly.

00:12:39.764 --> 00:12:41.180
That's basically
the type of thing

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.520
that you would use as
a computer scientist,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.800
and you want to embed
some machine learning

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:48.330
into a physical device.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:50.510
It's unlikely as
an R user you will

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:52.940
touch TF Estimators very much.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:54.770
But then we also
have supporting tools

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:59.180
to make it possible to get your
data into the required format.

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:02.590
TF Data Sets gives you
scalable input to pipelines.

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:06.720
TF Runs I will talk about
in a little bit more detail.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.470
It gives you a great way
of running your TensorFlow

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:12.100
experiments in a systematic way.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:17.070
And TF Deploy enables you
to publish your train model

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:23.180
onto either RStudio Connect
or into the Google Cloud ML

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:24.710
service.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:29.870
And Cloud ML is a
porch or a great way

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.660
of accessing the Cloud
ML services on Google.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:35.270
And I would like to demonstrate
some of that for you live.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.670
So, why would you do this?

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:43.430
So if you're short on ideas
about why you should care

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:45.170
about this, we have
some great examples

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:48.330
on our gallery at RStudio.

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:50.210
The classical
examples of TensorFlow

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.520
are for complex
perceptual problems

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:59.150
as Mikhail said earlier, so
image classification, research

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:03.770
in cancer, immunotherapy, credit
card fraud detection, machine

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:08.830
translation, these types of
complex perceptual problems.

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:10.730
And typically,
people will tell you

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:13.200
that you need very
large amount of data

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:14.910
for that to be sensible.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:17.270
I will actually
demonstrate much more

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:20.646
of a toy example of something
that fits in my laptop very,

00:14:20.646 --> 00:14:21.145
very easily.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:26.290
And just to illustrate
the point that you

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:30.500
don't need to have a million
images for TensorFlow

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:31.120
to make sense.

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:34.400
You can actually use TensorFlow
on traditional machine learning

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:35.570
problems.

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:39.460
I'm not saying that TensorFlow
is going to outperform Xgboost.

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.630
Or if you have structured
data, that's probably not

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:43.230
going to be the case.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:48.940
But it has a place in
these mixed environments.

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:51.310
So, let's talk briefly
about the steps

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:53.170
in building a Keras model.

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:57.430
And unsurprisingly,
these steps are exactly

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:02.350
the same steps you would take
for pretty much every machine

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:03.760
learning problem anyway.

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:06.610
Maybe the compilation
step is a bit different.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.800
I'll show some code, but
here's the highlight.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:13.150
First of all, you
define your model.

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:14.800
Typically that'll be
a sequential model

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.660
where your layers follow
sequentially one on the other.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:21.190
That is the majority
of examples you'll

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:25.300
see are layers that just
sequentially follow on.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:28.750
But there's also
a functional model

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:32.920
that allows you to combine
different neural networks

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.100
if you have more
complicated problems.

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:39.550
And Keras allows you
to have multiple GPUs.

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:44.200
So you can run your code on
not just a single machine,

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:47.200
but also on classes
of GPU very easily.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:50.560
Once you've set up the model,
then there's a very simple step

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:55.420
of compilation that compiles the
code via Python into the native

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:57.520
C++.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:00.160
And for that step, you'll
define your optimizer, your loss

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.110
function, and the metrics
you want to measure.

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:03.943
Typically, it'll be
your validation accuracy

00:16:03.943 --> 00:16:06.080
or something similar.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:07.950
Then you will actually
fit the model.

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:12.210
Originally in R we would
call this just a train.

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.110
In Keras, it's called Fits.

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:18.490
You'll do your evaluation on
how well your accuracy is doing.

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:22.570
And maybe you'll do some plots
to evaluate your accuracy

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:24.790
on several intervals.

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:28.300
And then you'll protect either
your classes or repeatability.

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:29.470
We have a cheat sheet.

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:31.946
And I have-- if you search
for "Keras Cheat Sheet,"

00:16:31.946 --> 00:16:33.070
at RStudio, you'll find it.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.930
But there's the link as well.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:37.990
And Chrestkha, I think
it's time for a quick demo.

00:16:42.256 --> 00:16:43.175
Hopefully that works.

00:16:43.175 --> 00:16:43.760
All right.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:47.180
So first of all,
you are actually

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:50.150
looking at an instance of
RStudio Studio Server running

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:53.380
on a virtual machine
in Google Cloud ML.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:56.990
So standard RStudio
Server and the back end.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:58.880
And Mikhail and I spent
some time on Monday

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:07.070
to just install or point
to NVIDIA GPU processes

00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:07.819
onto this machine.

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:09.770
So we have GPU available.

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:12.140
And I'm going to run
some code just so you

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:14.180
can see how the
integration works

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:17.960
and just to make sure that
there's no jiggery proqui.

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:19.595
Let me just restart my session.

00:17:19.595 --> 00:17:22.349
Hold on, clean slate.

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:25.980
So, let me set the scene.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:31.160
I have a bit of code in that
takes some time series data.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:34.170
And the data was--

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:37.380
originates from
15 people wearing

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:39.180
a chest-mounted accelerometer.

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:41.550
And this accelerometer
measures acceleration

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:44.170
in x, y, and z direction.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:47.070
And they were then told to
do different activities--

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:48.960
walking, and
running, and sitting,

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:52.250
going up and down
stairs, et cetera.

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.000
The original task,
which you can then--

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.630
you can find the data on the UCI
David Machine Learning website.

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:01.230
The original task
was to predict what

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.060
activity is this person doing?

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:05.970
We've slightly flipped
it in this example.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:09.660
And I'm saying, I know that
this person is walking.

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:11.910
From the trace from
the accelerometer,

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:16.370
can I determine which person
is wearing the device?

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:17.790
A small set, it's
only 15 people.

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:18.540
So I don't think--

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:20.370
I'm not claiming
that this will work

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:21.630
if you have a million people.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:22.879
Like this probably won't work.

00:18:22.879 --> 00:18:26.010
But in this case, a toy example,
I think it's quite nice.

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:28.070
I'm not going to run
through the code right here.

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:30.030
Mechanical steps
with the code, I'm

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:37.320
going to just run it in
one consecutive session.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.370
And I want you to
observe just two things.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.750
One is there will be some
red text that just floats up

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:43.740
momentarily.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.290
And if you look
very carefully, it's

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:50.220
TensorFlow communicating
back to the R session saying,

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:51.660
I'm running on a GPU.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.220
You may just see
that flashing past.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:57.690
And then once the
training starts,

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.330
we have interactive
visualization

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.470
in the RStudio IDE.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:04.820
So for every epoch, for
every full iteration

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:09.810
through all of the data, it
will update a plot in the IDE

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:13.330
that gives you instantaneous
feedback on what's happening.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:18.020
So let's see if this works.

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:19.110
Go to Starting.

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:23.690
There's the TensorFlow messages
saying, I'm running on GPU.

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:25.563
And now it's starting the train.

00:19:25.563 --> 00:19:31.080
And there we have the
interactive plot flashing up.

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:33.980
I think it's time to be
about once every second

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.780
or so it will update.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:39.920
And I can look at this
plot very briefly.

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:43.500
The top plot shows me my loss.

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:47.790
And the bottom plot
shows me my accuracy.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:50.610
Blue is my training accuracy.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:52.150
And green is my
validation accuracy.

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:54.990
So this is a nicely
behaved model.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:58.190
There's no big discrepancy
between validation

00:19:58.190 --> 00:19:59.230
and training.

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:01.730
So this is a nicely
behaved model.

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:04.920
So if I now switch
back into the slides,

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:09.330
I can just briefly give
you a bit more flavor

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:10.930
on what's happening.

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:13.290
So if you want to actually
write some R code,

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:15.810
I just want to give some
pointers about things

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:19.380
you should be careful about.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:22.820
The first is that we have--

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:25.140
there's this funny
operator here.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:29.010
I'm not even sure
what you call it.

00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:32.200
It's a reverse byte, right?

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:34.430
See, that looks like a
magrittr pipe for those of you

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:36.220
who are familiar with
magrittr or dplyr.

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:38.610
But it points the other way.

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:42.690
And what this operator
does it gives you

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:47.340
a way to mimic simultaneous
assignments of objects in R.

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:50.760
So this is something you can
do in Python very easily.

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:55.800
You can say, x comma
y equals 1 comma 2.

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:57.970
And you assign x and
y simultaneously.

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:01.410
This operator allows me
to simultaneously sign

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:04.890
x_train, y_train,
x_test, y_test, which

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:09.550
are embedded objects in this
data set and list object.

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:13.410
So that's the first thing you
should just take note of that.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.465
The second important
thing is Array Reshape.

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:22.020
This is important in the
context of Python with SSR.

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:25.200
So you all-- pretty much
everybody said I'm an R user.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:28.500
So you will know that
in R, vectors are--

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:32.020
or matrices are
columnar, column first.

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:34.570
But in most other
programming languages,

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:39.440
including Python, the
arrays are row primary.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:45.720
So you have to use Array Reshape
to get your data in the format

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:47.520
that TensorFlow understands.

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:50.070
Do not try and use
the Dim function in R.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:51.270
That will not work.

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:54.860
So it just-- top pro tip,
just use Array Reshape.

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:59.440
Then the next line I have
here that's interesting

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:03.250
is dividing x_train by 255.

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:06.720
I'm just rescaling all my
values in a range 0 to 1.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:09.600
It's very important in
TensorFlow and Keras

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:13.040
to have your input values
scaled to the same value,

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:15.720
and you have to-- or
scale for the same range.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:19.290
And that range should be
minus 1 to plus 1, or 0 to 1.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:23.970
If you don't do that, you
may get numerical convergence

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:25.440
problems.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:29.680
So that's something
that's in most R packages

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.580
that that algorithm
will take care for you.

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:34.320
In Keras, you have to do
a bit more work yourself.

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:37.350
And then there are two
functions, two categorical

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:42.990
and some other functions that
helps you convert your factor

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:47.360
levels to in machine-- in
the computer science world,

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:49.230
it's called one hot encoding.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:52.020
In statistics, we call
it dummy encoding.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:54.090
So you have to use
this two categorical

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:57.070
function to make that work.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:22:59.430
And next, then I'm
defining a model.

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:01.850
My model, in this
case, I have just

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.530
four layers-- a drop-out layer,
a dense layer, and so on.

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:06.850
I'm not going to
explain that right now.

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:09.810
You can find that in any
[INAUDIBLE] tutorial.

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:11.420
But that's fairly simple to do.

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:14.650
Again, just notice the pipe
function that we have in R.

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:21.922
It is a very natural way to code
in R. Then you have to compile.

00:23:21.922 --> 00:23:25.550
A simple step, one thing you
have to be careful about here,

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:32.330
do not assign the value
back to your model.

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:34.400
So if you're interested
in the technical detail,

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:38.550
this is because this Keras
object is an R6 class.

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:41.680
So it's doing
modification by reference.

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:46.320
If you accidentally reassign
the value at this step,

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:48.095
you'll get some very
strange results.

00:23:48.095 --> 00:23:50.410
So pro tip, don't do that.

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:52.760
And then we want-- now
we're ready to train.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:54.710
The function is called fit.

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:58.460
We begin to assign that
object so you're not--

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:01.340
or the result's in
optical history, which

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:04.160
means that I can plot that,
and I can inspect that.

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:08.600
And if you simply called
plots on that history object,

00:24:08.600 --> 00:24:11.060
you get a nice ggplot object
with the same information

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:15.070
that I showed you earlier
in the dynamic plot.

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:19.760
That brief very, very quick
overview of how Keras works.

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:21.980
Let me introduce you to
one of the other packages.

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:24.620
Remember the table I had
earlier on the right-hand side?

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:26.420
We have some supporting tools.

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:31.040
TF Runs short-- or
reactions TensorFlow Runs

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:35.640
is, I think, a fantastic way
to manage your experiments.

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:38.340
And each experiment is a run.

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:42.440
And really, the only
thing you need to remember

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:47.720
is that there's a function
called Training Run, which

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:52.700
is similar to Source in R.
So in R, you would say Source

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:54.920
and runs the entire script.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:58.535
If you do Training Run, it
will source the entire script

00:24:58.535 --> 00:24:59.270
and file.

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:00.940
But it will do some
bookkeeping for you.

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:07.130
It will remember every run,
what the hyperparameters were

00:25:07.130 --> 00:25:07.910
that you used.

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:09.030
What was the exact code?

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:12.190
It will put that into a
small local version control

00:25:12.190 --> 00:25:14.180
so can go back and compare.

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:17.540
And it also captures all
your output, your validation

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:19.980
accuracy, your training
accuracy, and so on.

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.120
So you can easily inspect
after the fact what's

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:25.490
happened by just
querying it a data

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:28.592
frame with that information.

00:25:28.592 --> 00:25:32.490
So top tip, go and use TF Runs.

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:35.610
And actually at this
point, I can just

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:41.470
give a very quick demo
of TF Runs in practice.

