00:38:42.630 --> 00:38:44.910
and this is a topic I'm
passionate about-- again,

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:47.940
deploying those models
for consumption.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:52.380
Again, the UI from the-- oops--

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:55.720
from the Google Cloud
Platform, it's very simple.

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:58.840
But the idea is once you
register those models,

00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:00.960
they're available in
a central repository

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:03.450
for your organization
to manage and maintain.

00:39:03.450 --> 00:39:05.730
Again, you can follow
your own taxonomy.

00:39:05.730 --> 00:39:08.040
Here, I've just published
a few sample models.

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:10.440
They might be relevant
to certain functions,

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:12.060
certain business lines.

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:14.610
I have a couple here that
maybe the digital marketing

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:17.287
team has a model to predict
click-through rates.

00:39:17.287 --> 00:39:18.870
We have the computer
vision department

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:23.430
that's trying to recognize the
image of the product catalog,

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:25.230
some text classification.

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:27.840
So all these models are
now available for REST API

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:29.560
consumption.

00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:33.100
I ran a loop to
actually-- oh, sorry--

00:39:33.100 --> 00:39:37.140
to actually see-- oops.

00:39:40.550 --> 00:39:43.620
I ran a couple of calls to
the image classification model

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:44.490
last night.

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:47.610
And what this allows you to do
from a monitoring standpoint

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:49.720
is maybe take a look
at the last 12 hours.

00:39:55.180 --> 00:39:58.440
And it really gives you a lot
of information around, how

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:00.810
often is the model being
used by your applications,

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:01.950
by end users?

00:40:01.950 --> 00:40:03.540
What does the
predictions per second?

00:40:03.540 --> 00:40:05.220
From a performance
standpoint, also

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:08.640
being able to track latency and
issues, along with the logging,

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:11.520
to have a nice way to see--
you can see, a lot of times

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:12.750
we have a pretty low latency.

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:14.100
But there are some
peaks during the night

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:16.709
here when I ran the loop where
that really could be a problem.

00:40:16.709 --> 00:40:18.750
You can debug and make
sure these predictions are

00:40:18.750 --> 00:40:22.140
serving correctly whether you
need real-time predictions

00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:23.700
or at batch.

00:40:23.700 --> 00:40:26.790
I think we wanted to show the
Shiny examples as a front end,

00:40:26.790 --> 00:40:30.002
and then we're
going to wrap it up.

00:40:30.002 --> 00:40:31.480
ANDRIE DE VRIES:
Thanks, Mikhail.

00:40:31.480 --> 00:40:36.710
OK, so as Mikhail suggested,
we want to deploy this.

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:40.770
And he said earlier, you
can deploy these models

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:42.900
as APIs in the service.

00:40:42.900 --> 00:40:47.340
You can do the same thing, you
can deploy TensorFlow models

00:40:47.340 --> 00:40:51.360
as an API in RStudio Connect,
which is an [INAUDIBLE] product

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:54.200
that gives you a publication
platform to publish your Shiny

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:56.490
apps or monitor
reports, et cetera.

00:40:56.490 --> 00:41:01.030
And I want to show you
one example of that

00:41:01.030 --> 00:41:04.890
in RStudio Connect live.

00:41:04.890 --> 00:41:07.890
This is a small toy
example I wrote.

00:41:07.890 --> 00:41:09.990
It uses a pre-trained
Keras model.

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:11.730
So I didn't do any
training myself.

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:14.850
I used a publicly
available model

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:16.830
that is fairly
sophisticated, not--

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:18.210
it's a number of years old.

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:20.610
You can get better
models these days.

00:41:20.610 --> 00:41:22.890
And what it does
is I can tell it

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:28.272
to upload a small image
perhaps of my dog.

00:41:28.272 --> 00:41:31.500
And then once the
image is uploaded,

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:35.220
it will try and tell
me what's there.

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:37.770
It thinks my dog is a
Malamute, or a Collie,

00:41:37.770 --> 00:41:39.980
maybe an Eskimo dog.

00:41:39.980 --> 00:41:44.450
Let's tell it to give me more
categories, 10 categories.

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:46.860
It definitely thinks
it's some kind of dog.

00:41:46.860 --> 00:41:48.147
It's none of those.

00:41:48.147 --> 00:41:49.480
It's actually Finnish Lapponian.

00:41:49.480 --> 00:41:51.590
But if you know what
a Malamute looks like,

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:53.830
it's a pretty good guess.

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:55.920
The code itself is very simple.

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:58.740
It's only about 50
lines of code in total.

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:02.780
And the key element of that
code is in this line here.

00:42:02.780 --> 00:42:08.280
The application raised at 50
is both in function and Keras.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:10.330
Keras comes with some
pre-trained models,

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:13.300
including [INAUDIBLE],, and
exception, and exception,

00:42:13.300 --> 00:42:14.760
and mobile net.

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:19.666
And all I did was to say
use that model and just

00:42:19.666 --> 00:42:21.890
score on my own image.

00:42:21.890 --> 00:42:24.600
So there's my scoring
function, the product image.

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:28.140
It's basically-- it's
just model then predict,

00:42:28.140 --> 00:42:31.570
and then I decode
the predictions.

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:35.820
And that plot prediction,
that's the bar chart.

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:37.060
And that's it.

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:40.500
That's the entire application

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:45.350
If I upload a second
image maybe of my cat,

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:48.351
let's see what it thinks.

00:42:48.351 --> 00:42:49.975
Well, it thinks it's
some kind of cat--

00:42:49.975 --> 00:42:51.760
Tiger cat, or Egyptian cat.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:54.189
I think it's just
a Tabby cat really.

00:42:54.189 --> 00:42:56.001
[ANDRIE LAUGHS]

00:42:56.980 --> 00:42:59.250
So this is a little
toy example just

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:04.050
to illustrate that you can use
TensorFlow in your Shiny app.

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:07.880
And actually in
this case, I'm just

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:09.420
using CPU in the background.

00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:14.190
I don't even have a GPU to
serve the scoring function.

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:17.820
So you may get away
with not having

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:20.280
a very sophisticated machine.

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:21.090
I see some laughs.

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:23.400
That's probably because it
thinks, well, maybe it's

00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:25.086
bucket or a plastic bag.

00:43:25.086 --> 00:43:28.330
[ANDRIE LAUGHS]

00:43:28.330 --> 00:43:31.320
Go figure.

00:43:31.320 --> 00:43:32.850
The perils of deep
learning, don't

00:43:32.850 --> 00:43:35.110
assume that these things are
intelligent, they're not,

00:43:35.110 --> 00:43:36.430
right?

00:43:36.430 --> 00:43:42.700
They will tell you based on
what your training data set was.

